WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.735
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:06.070
I take App Engine's new
scheduling flags for a ride.

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.872
Will we buck the
performance trend?

00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:08.830
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:16.000
Now, one of the nice aspects
of "the cloud" being a platform

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.700
is that over time it'll
grow, change, and improve

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.970
itself, which is exactly why
we're talking about App Engine

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.150
instance count again.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.020
See, since our episode last
year, the App Engine team

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.910
rolled out a brand
new scheduling system

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.000
to help you get even more
control over how and when App

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.920
Engine spins up instances.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.410
So of course, I wanted to
dive in, kick the tires,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.850
and see how some of
the new settings works.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.760
But first, I wanted
to get a baseline read

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.430
on how the scheduler
responds in the same tests

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.990
that we used last time.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.610
Once again, our server
code is pretty simple.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.480
It's just a simulation of a
CPU intensive application.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.060
Obviously, if you have a
four-second response time,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.280
you've got bigger
problems to worry about.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.680
As a baseline,
here is 1k queries

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.410
per second, which the previous
scheduler shows differences

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.310
between the new one.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.496
We can see that without
touching any settings,

00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:04.870
the new scheduler is already
performing just a little bit

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.750
better for my contrived
scenario, which is good,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.000
because it means that
without lifting a finger

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.640
we get a little better
on instance counts.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.010
Next, is something I've been
interested in for a while--

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.620
min and max_instance settings.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.914
Max instances, of course,
defines the upper limit

00:01:19.914 --> 00:01:21.580
on the number of
instances you can have,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.920
which is helpful to
control your costs,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.400
while min instances ensures that
after deployment there's always

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.170
a certain number of
instances running.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.090
But before you set
this flag, make

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.640
sure you take into account how
your minimum instances count

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.190
interacts with your
idle instances count,

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.260
since they are two
very different concepts

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.051
and you don't want a
lot of spare instances

00:01:40.051 --> 00:01:41.891
lying around when
you don't need them.

00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:44.140
Make sure you check out the
article in the description

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.340
below for more details on that.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.870
Anyhow, next up is the target
throughput utilization flag,

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.410
which sets a threshold for the
number of concurrent requests,

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.360
after which more
instances will be

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.130
started to handle the traffic.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.000
This flag works hand in hand
with max_concurrent_requests

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.270
to determine your true number
of requests per instance.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.580
For throughput-heavy
applications,

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.910
lowering this value will result
in more instances being spun up

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.810
to handle the work, while
increasing this value

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.280
will let each instance better
handle the connections,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.370
and in most cases should
reduce instance count.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.190
Now, finally is the target
CPU utilization flag.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:25.490
This flag defines the
CPU utilization ratio,

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.720
which defines when a new
instance should be spun up.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.300
Or perhaps a better way to say
it, if your app is CPU bound,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.110
then this is the
parameter you tweak

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.340
to optimize between
performance and costs,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.496
since lowering and
raising it can impact

00:02:38.496 --> 00:02:40.120
how many instances
you spin up in order

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.560
to handle the workload.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.800
With an improved scheduler
and a new set of settings,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.730
App Engine gives you
much better controls

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.730
to better balance between
performance and costs,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.160
which in my humble
opinion is always

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.495
the spot you want to be in.

00:02:52.495 --> 00:02:54.370
If you'd like to know
more about these tests,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.450
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.150
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.950
Performance Atlas
content, because when

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.619
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:03:02.619 --> 00:03:06.264
[MUSIC PLAYING]

